Unfortunately I don't believe log_mix is vectorized. But you can vectorize the beta_binomial that you use later in your model. Instead of

for (i in 1:J) increment_log_prob( beta_binomial_log(Y[i],R[i],alpha[ gene_index[i] ],beta[ gene_index[i] ]) );

you can do something like

model {
  real alphas[J];
  real betas[J];

// ... other stuff in model block

  for (j in 1:J) {
    alphas[j] <- alpha[gene_index[j]];
    betas[j] <- beta[gene_index[j]];
  }
  increment_log_prob(beta_binomial_log(Y, R, alphas, betas)); // or Y ~ beta_binomial(R, alpha, beta)
}

which should be faster than putting the beta_binomial_log within the loop.

