messages.body_plain:
Any feedback you can give me on what may be conceptually wrong with this model, or what to try going forward would be great.  Actually, I would even be willing to pay someone money to work on this with me, so let me know if you are interested.  Thanks!

The goal of this model is to predict the second half score of an NBA game given that it's already at halftime.  The information used to predict the outcome is the Vegas full game spread which is publicly available before the game starts.  Also used is the score of the first half, the idea being that the more a team is winning the in first half the less well they will do in the second half, and vice versa.  I know for a fact that both of these are significant predictors which come close to predicting the second half outcome as best as it can be predicted without overfitting.

My hypothesis is that different teams have different tendencies in the second half in certain situations.  I suspect that these tendencies are minor, but may be enough to gain a little edge in accuracy of predictions.

So for instance, when the Cavaliers start off losing, I suspect they may be extra good (by .5 or 1 point compared to other teams on average) at making a comeback.  So to build my model, I use a hierarchical model based on teams and also three categories (3: performing extra well in the first half, 2: performing at expectation in the first half, 1: performing under expectation in the first half)

Here is my model (there are 30 teams, 3 categoryh1.  line refers to the vegas full game line, and diffh1 refers to how much the away team is winning first half)

model {
  for (i in 1:Ntotal) {
    resulth2[i] ~ dnorm(mu[i], 1 / (sigma ^ 2))
    mu[i] <- beta0[team[i], categoryh1[i]] + beta1[team[i], categoryh1[i]] * line[i] + beta2[team[i], categoryh1[i]] * diffh1[i]
  }

  for (j in 1:Nsubj) {
    for (z in 1:3) {
      beta0[j, z] ~ dnorm( beta0mu, 1)
      beta1[j, z] ~ dnorm( beta1mu, 1)
      beta2[j, z] ~ dnorm( beta2mu, 1)
    }
  }

  beta0mu  ~ dnorm( 0, 1)
  beta1mu  ~ dnorm( 0, 1 )
  beta2mu  ~ dnorm( 0, 1 )
  sigma ~ dnorm(1,1)
}"

I've tried this model along with changing the prior from 1 to 10, 50, 100 and none seem to be successful.

I use 600 games to train on and 200 games to test on (and do this for a couple different seasons).  Using beta0, beta1, beta3, the teams, and categoryh1, I predict the results in the 200 games and compare them to what the Vegas line was for the second half.  Anywhere between accuracy 52%-56% is the desired result.  Below that and the model is basically like flipping a coin.  Above that and the win rate is too high to be believable.  After backtesting it is clear this model does not work it needs some improvement!

Inspired by Andrew Gelman’s World cup model <http://andrewgelman.com/2014/07/13/stan-analyzes-world-cup-data/>.I have been trying to translate the following football model by Baio and Blangiardo <http://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/02664760802684177> to stan. In the end I would like to reproduce Table 2 from the paper.

My model takes the following form (file "football.stan"):
data {
  int nteams;
  int ngames;
  vector[nteams] prior_score;
  int team_home[ngames];
  int team_away[ngames];
  int score_home[ngames];
  int score_away[ngames];
}

transformed data {
}

parameters {
  real home;
  real sig_att;
  real mu_att;
  real sig_def;
  real mu_def;
  vector[nteams - 1] att_free;
  vector[nteams - 1] def_free;
}

transformed parameters {
  vector[nteams] att;
  vector[nteams] def;
  vector[ngames] theta_home;
  vector[ngames] theta_away;

  # Constrain sum(att) = 0, sum(def) = 0
  # see: https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/issues/1202
  for (k in 1:(nteams - 1)) {
    att[k] <- att_free[k];
    def[k] <- def_free[k];
  }
  att[nteams] <- -sum(att_free);
  def[nteams] <- -sum(def_free);

  for (i in 1:ngames) {
    theta_home[i] <- exp(home + att[team_home[i]] + def[team_away[i]]);
    theta_away[i] <- exp(att[team_away[i]] + def[team_home[i]]);
  }
}

model {
# Priors
  home ~ normal(0, 0.0001);

  mu_att ~ normal(0, 0.0001);
  mu_def ~ normal(0, 0.0001);
  sig_att ~ gamma(0.1, 0.1);
  sig_def ~ gamma(0.1, 0.1);

  for (i in 1:(nteams - 1)) {
    att_free[i] ~ normal(0, sig_att);
    def_free[i] ~ normal(0, sig_def);
  }

  for (i in 1:ngames) {
    score_home[i] ~ poisson(theta_home[i]);
    score_away[i] ~ poisson(theta_away[i]);
  }
}

I run stan from within R using rstan
library("rstan");
d <- read.csv("seria_a_1991_1992.csv", header = TRUE);
d$score_home <- d[, 3];
d$score_away <- d[, 4];
d$idx_home <- d[, 5] + 1;
d$idx_away <- d[, 6] + 1;
teams <- as.character(sort(unique(d[, 1])));
nTeams <- length(teams);

data <- list(nteams = nTeams,
             ngames = nrow(d),
             prior_score = rep(1, nTeams),
             team_home = d$idx_home,
             team_away = d$idx_away,
             score_home = d$score_home,
             score_away = d$score_away);

stan_fit <- stan("football.stan",
data = data,
iter = 2000,
chains = 4);

plot(stan_fit, pars = c("home",
                   "mu_att", "sig_att",
                   "mu_def", "sig_def",
                   "att[1]", "att[2]", "att[3]",
                   "att[4]", "att[5]", "att[6]"));

A look at the top 6 attack parameters att[1],…,att[6] seems to give a reasonable match with the first 6 entries from Table 2. What puzzles me is the fact that stan always seems to set parameters home, mu_att and mu_def to zero. I have used the same priors as in the paper.

Could somebody explain what is going on here, and why e.g. parameter home does not seem to have any affect in my stan model? I must be making some mistake in including the model parameters.

On a site note: Baio and Blangiardo refer to their choice of (hyper-)priors ~normal(0, 0.0001) as "flat priors”. Could somebody explain why this would be considered a flat prior? Surely normal(0, 0.0001) is not really flat?

Thanks,

Maurits

PS. I am attaching the full model with data and R script.

Stan uses the scale (std dev) parameterization of
normal, like R, so this is probably not what you
meant:

  home ~ normal(0, 0.0001);

Those diffuse gamma priors can also be problematic; we
usually recommend half-Cauchy or even half-normal.

Also, you can vectorize to make this all much much
faster and the code much simpler. This:

 for (i in 1:ngames) {
   theta_home[i] <- exp(home + att[team_home[i]] + def[team_away[i]]);
   theta_away[i] <- exp(att[team_away[i]] + def[team_home[i]]);
 }

 for (i in 1:ngames) {
   score_home[i] ~ poisson(theta_home[i]);
   score_away[i] ~ poisson(theta_away[i]);
 }

can be replaced with:

 for (i in 1:ngames) {

   log_theta_home[i] <- home + att[team_home[i]] + def[team_away[i]];
   log_theta_away[i] <- att[team_away[i]] + def[team_home[i]];
 }

  score_home ~ poisson_log(log_theta_home);
  score_away ~ poisson_log(log_theta_away);

The poisson_log function takes its parameter on the log scale.

With our new array slicing and indexing, both of these can
be replaced with the two-liner:

  score_home ~ poisson_log(home + att[team_home] + def[team_away]);
  score_away ~ poisson_log(att[team_away] + def[team_home]);

This isn't even in the manual yet --- I need to update it
ASAP with examples!

- Bob

