> On Mar 7, 2016, at 4:48 PM, Maurits Evers <maurit...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Dear Bob.
>
> Thanks for the fast reply and for pointing out the issue with the normal prior. Indeed Baio and Blangiardo parameterise their normal priors in terms of mean and precision (1/sigma^2). I should've read their paper more carefully and picked that up.
>
> Upon changing the priors for home, mu_att and mu_def in stan to
> normal(0, 1e5)

That might as well be flat.  We recommend more informative
priors if you have information about it.  I somehow don't think
1e5 is a reasonable number here if we're dealing with football
scores.

> I get a non-zero estimate for home. That also eliminates my previous question about flat priors. All good!
>
> Stan-derived estimates for mu_att and mu_def are not informative,

If the model's right and Stan converges, these will be *the* Bayesian posterior
mean estimates give or take MCMC std error.

> for example mean(mu_att) +- sd(mu_att) = 696 +- 99762.
> I was expecting stan to drive mu_att and mu_def to zero due to shrinkage. Could somebody point out why this is not the case?

Well, that looks pretty close to zero on its scale if you didn't
skip any decimal points.  Posterior densities are only as
tight as the data allows.

> Also, thanks Bob for tidying up my stan model code; I was aware of certain vectorisations; others are new to me ("array slicing and indexing"). I usually leave for-loops in my stan models to help with (initial) readability and debugging.

It's less error prone to vectorize once you get used to it.
We have that issue in deciding how to present models the
first time to users.

> One more question about the vectorisation and code optimisation: Let's say I would like to store estimates for parameters theta_home and theta_away; would it be faster to do what you did (i.e. use the poisson_log) and then inverse-transform the log_theta's back to the linear scale in a separate generated quantities block?

Yes.  Use the log-scale distributions for sampling --- they're
more arithmetically stable and faster.

You can regenerate the values in generated quantities,
which is fast because there are no gradients involved.

- Bob

